{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgHvmj1uKRX6vhmkzW6QG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tomorrow the test still passes. It does not read `tracker.json` at all. It writes a frozen copy of today's 5 issues (3 open) to a temporary file and calls `weekly_report()` on that file. So the hourly sync can't break it.\n\nI checked this. I copied the code to a scratch folder and replaced `tracker.json` there with a different file that has 1 open issue. Both tests still passed. My first try showed an error, but the cause was a flag I used (`-I`), which stopped Python from finding `report.py`. Without it, the tests passed.\n\nThere is a cost to this. The test proves that `weekly_report()` counts correctly. It does not say anything about the live file. If the export format changes (for example, `\"state\"` becomes `\"status\"`, or open issues are marked `\"Open\"`), the live report could be wrong and this test would still pass.\n\nWhat did you want the test to cover?\n\n- **A. Counting logic only** (what I built, and my default): the test is stable, and it never sees the real export.\n- **B. The live file each time it runs:** the test reads the real `tracker.json`. It can't expect a fixed 3, because that number changes every hour. It could check that the file loads, that each issue has a `\"state\"` field with a value it knows, and that the report's number matches a count it makes on its own.\n- **C. Both A and B.**"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":320,"cache_read_input_tokens":41372,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":320},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d55ebbd7-38ed-4e9c-8276-0fc62dc681a7","uuid":"9645dc15-aa43-4fbb-947a-f